  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints **27.06**.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turned `\"12.5\"` into 12 dollars and `\"05\"` cents, so 1205 instead of 1250. The quote then came out as 2 \u00d7 12.05 plus 8.25% tax = 26.09. Any price with a single decimal digit was affected.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. I also dropped the `or 0`, because `ljust` already turns an empty string into `\"00\"`. Checks: `\"12\"` \u2192 1200, `\"12.5\"` \u2192 1250, `\"12.05\"` \u2192 1205, `\"0.5\"` \u2192 50.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`: all 6 pass. None of the existing tests cover `to_cents`, which is how this slipped through. Adding a test for one-decimal prices would be worthwhile.\n\n**Separate bug, not fixed:** negative amounts with a decimal part are also wrong. `to_cents(\"-1.5\")` gives -50 instead of -150, because the cents are added to a negative whole number. The CLI probably never receives negative prices, so I left it alone.",
